## 3. Proposed proof of positivity at the limit

Fix \(\varepsilon,h,\sigma,\delta>0\) and work on the event

\[
E_{\varepsilon,\delta}
=
\{Q\le-\varepsilon\}\cap\{|a|\le\delta\}\cap\{|w|\le\delta\}\cap\{|z|\le\delta\}.
\]

Let \(R=\{-1/2+\sigma\le x\le 1/2-\sigma\}\cap\{|y|\le h\}\).

**(i) The horizontal faces are inflowing.** On \(y=+h\), for \(|x|\le1/2\) so that \(|x^2-1/4|\le1/4\):

\[
P_y
\le
\frac\delta2\cdot\frac14-\varepsilon h+\delta h\cdot\frac12+\frac\delta2h^2
=
\delta\left(\frac18+\frac h2+\frac{h^2}2\right)-\varepsilon h .
\]

With \(h\le1\) the bracket is at most \(9/8\), so \(P_y<0\) whenever

\[
\delta<\frac{8\varepsilon h}{9}.
\]

Ascent flow therefore pushes \(y\) downward at the top face; symmetrically upward at \(y=-h\). The strip \(|y|\le h\) is forward-invariant.

**(ii) \(x\) decreases monotonically across the strip.** On \(R\), \(x^2-1/4\le-\sigma+\sigma^2\), so

\[
P_x\le-\sigma+\sigma^2+\frac{\delta h}2+\frac{\delta h^2}2 ,
\]

which is negative whenever

\[
\delta<\frac{2\sigma(1-\sigma)}{h+h^2}.
\]

So \(\dot x<0\) throughout \(R\): once inside, the orbit moves left at a rate bounded below, and cannot exit right.

**(iii) Only one critical point is reachable.** For \(\delta\) small the critical points of \(P\) are \(C^1\)-perturbations of \((\pm1/2,0)\) by the implicit function theorem (the unperturbed Hessians are invertible when \(Q\le-\varepsilon\)). Inside \(\{x<1/2-\sigma\}\cap\{|y|\le h\}\) the only one is the perturbed \(M\), still a sink since \(H\prec0\) is an open condition.

**(iv) Conclusion on \(E_{\varepsilon,\delta}\).** The leftward unstable branch of \(S\) enters \(R\), is trapped in \(|y|\le h\) by (i), moves monotonically left by (ii), and its \(\omega\)-limit is a critical point with \(P\) strictly above \(P(S)\) — necessarily the perturbed \(M\) by (iii). **\(M\) and \(S\) are gradient adjacent.**

**(v) An explicit constant.** Because \((Q,a,w,z)\) are independent,

\[
a_0
\ \ge\
P(E_{\varepsilon,\delta})
=
\Phi\!\left(\frac{b-\varepsilon}{\sqrt2}\right)
\left[2\Phi\!\left(\frac\delta{\sqrt2}\right)-1\right]^2
\left[2\Phi\!\left(\frac\delta{\sqrt6}\right)-1\right].
\]

At \(b=6/5\), \(\varepsilon=0.1\), \(h=1\), \(\sigma=0.1\), \(\delta=0.05\) — which satisfies both constraints, \(0.05<8(0.1)(1)/9=0.0889\) and \(0.05<2(0.1)(0.9)/2=0.09\):

\[
\Phi(0.7778)\approx0.7817,\quad
2\Phi(0.03536)-1\approx0.02821,\quad
2\Phi(0.02041)-1\approx0.01629,
\]

\[
a_0\ \gtrsim\ 1.0\times10^{-5}.
\]

